  "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={\u03a6}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}\u2061(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}\u2061(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}\u2061(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}\u2061(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}\u2061(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}\u2061(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
